  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This adapter is compiled only for unit tests. TermLike requires this\n callback, but our fixed-width, single-line displays do not move down.\n It is a no-op with no application behavior or untrusted-input path.\n Real terminal drawing is exercised by the PTY integration test."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_down]` is acceptable. It sits on an explicit function with a body, and the commentary gives a substantive reason, not just difficulty. Each claim matches the source I read:\n\n- **Test-only adapter:** `RecordingTerm` is inside `mod tests`, which is marked `#[cfg(test)]`.\n- **Single-line displays:** `Progress::with_target` builds only single-line templates, `{spinner} {msg} [{elapsed_precise}]` with an optional bar and count. Nothing in them moves the cursor down.\n- **No-op:** `move_cursor_down` just returns `Ok(())`. It has no application behavior and takes no untrusted input.\n- **PTY integration test:** `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[coverage_progress_on_a_pty_preserves_reports_and_plain_output_policy]` opens a real PTY with `openpty`. It asserts that the \"Converting coverage\" display and the \"1/1 attempts\" count appear and that the display is cleared. So real terminal drawing is exercised there.\n\nI found no gap.",
